# Catalog NUL-names investigation (OPEN)

There is an open, characterized-but-unsolved bug: the n1 production capture has
directory-name components in the on-disk catalog whose bytes are zeroed while
`name_len` stays correct (16,633 entries in `uid_shard_362.bin`, name lengths 32–54,
depth/parent clustered), plus duplicate directory entries (e.g. four `data1` dirs
under pid=1). Leading hypothesis: a zeroing event hitting jemalloc 48-byte
size-class runs (name buffers + hash-table entries), breaking HT chains.

Before changing catalog creation/writing/reading code, or starting any related
debugging or repro work, read `docs/catalog-nul-names-investigation.md` — it records
the established facts (do not re-derive them), what was already ruled out (reader
exonerated; ASan/jemalloc synthetic repros all clean), the fixes already landed, the
`ECRAWL_CAT_TRIPWIRE` instrumentation, and the ordered next steps.

If you learn something new about this bug, update that document in the same change.
